<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sana'a</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sana'a is an ancient city in the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and an important centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a>. In the sixth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumites</a> installed a Christian ruler there after their invasion of Himyar. According to the Aksumite Empire profile, a Sasanian expeditionary force later defeated the Aksumite garrison at Sana'a and installed a Persian governor, cutting Aksum off from the Arabian side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanaa.jpg" alt="Sana'a" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dan from Brussels, Europe · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sana%27a_(2286825748).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sanaa stands at about 2,300 metres above sea level, on a plain of the same name, the Haql Sanaa, between the mountains of Jabal An-Nabi Shu'ayb and Jabal Tiyal. The plain is roughly 50 to 60 km long from north to south and about 25 km wide, bordered by cliffs and mountains from which wadis descend. The Old City is known for its tower houses of stone and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fired-brick">fired brick</a>, which can rise to eight storeys. Their ground floors were traditionally used for grain and animals, and the top room, the mafraj, serves as a second reception room.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city was a secondary capital of the Kingdom of Saba, whose main capital was the oasis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib">Marib</a>. Its name is probably derived from a Sabaic root meaning well-fortified. From the time of Muhammad, around 622 CE, Sanaa was the governing seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> until independent sub-states appeared, and all of Yemen's states competed to control it.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> in 1538, under Suleiman, and conquered Sanaa in 1547 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ozdemir-pasha">Özdemir Pasha</a>. In 1602 the local Zaydi imams, led by Imam al-Mu'ayyad, reasserted their control, and Ottoman troops were forced out in 1629. In 1904, as Ottoman influence waned, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-muhammad-hamid-ed-din">Imam Yahya</a> took power in Sanaa. He followed a policy of isolation that left the city with little new infrastructure. Before the revolution of 1962 society was divided into five ranked groups, headed by the sayyids and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a>. The Jewish community, present in Yemen since the 5th century BCE, was expelled in 1679 and returned in 1680 to a quarter outside the walls. After 1948 about 49,000 of an estimated 51,000 Yemenite Jews were airlifted to Israel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sanaa is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, with a population of about 3,300,000 in 2023. It grew rapidly from the 1960s, when it became the capital of the republic and a new city spread beyond the old one. The Old City, enclosed by clay walls 9 to 14 metres high and holding more than 100 mosques, 12 hammams and 6,500 houses, was made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1986. The Great Mosque is more than 1,400 years old. The sung tradition al-Ghina al-San'ani, which dates to the 14th century, was designated UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage in 2003.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sanaa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Sasanian expeditionary force under the general Wahrez took <b>Sana'a</b>, overthrew the Abyssinian rulers of Yemen, the successors of Abraha, and installed Persian rule, which cut Aksum off from the eastern shore of the Red Sea and ended decades of commercial and strategic influence over the Arabian trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
